{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WB1ZMNqd54oH62uXb36Mb6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Executable Optimization Plan — L1/058 MoE Expert Token Radix Sort + Prefix Sum\n2\t\n3\tTarget: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Primary implementation = **Triton**; PyTorch only for\n4\ttensor allocation/metadata/launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational\n5\tfallback. Submission entry point: `solution/solution.py::run(topk_idx) -> (sorted_token_indices,\n6\texpert_offsets)`.\n7\t\n8\tThis plan operationalizes `docs/draft.md`. It defines: the fixed pipeline architecture, a sequential\n9\tcandidate lineage with per-candidate hypotheses and accept/reject rules, correctness checks, the\n10\tstopping criteria, and the evidence schema. **No candidate is implemented or evaluated in this\n11\tturn.**\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 0. Problem recap (authoritative constraints)\n16\t\n17\t- `run(topk_idx: int32[B, S, 8]) -> (sorted_token_indices: int32[N], expert_offsets: int32[257])`\n18\t  with `num_experts = 256`, `num_experts_per_tok = 8`, `N = B*S*8`.\n19\t- Semantics to reproduce **exactly** (integer, exact-match comparison; atol=1e-5/rtol=0.01 on ints):\n20\t  1. `flat = topk_idx.reshape(-1)` (C-order), length `N`, values in `[0,255]`.\n21\t  2. `sorted_token_indices = argsort(flat, stable=True)` — **stable** counting sort permutation:\n22\t     within each expert bucket the original flat indices appear in **strictly ascending** order.\n23\t  3. `expert_offsets[0] = 0`; `expert_offsets[e+1] = sum_{j<=e} count[j]` (inclusive prefix of the\n24\t     256-bin histogram). `expert_offsets[e]:expert_offsets[e+1]` = expert `e`'s output slice.\n25\t- Feedback workloads (5 fixed): `N ∈ {17920, 18432, 17408, 16384, 16384}` — all ~16–18k, keys\n26\t  uniform over 256 experts (mean bucket ≈ 64–72). Flat array ≈ 64–72 KB → fully L2-resident on A800.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## 1. Fixed pipeline architecture\n31\t\n32\tTwo logical stages with a hard cross-stage dependency (offsets must be complete before scatter can\n33\tcompute bucket bases), so ≥2 kernel launches are required.\n34\t\n35\t### Stage A — histogram + prefix sum → `expert_offsets[257]`\n36\tCompute the 256-bin histogram of `flat`, then an inclusive prefix sum, writing\n37\t`expert_offsets[0]=0` and `expert_offsets[1:257]=inclusive_cumsum`. Purely order-independent\n38\t(commutative counting), so any histogram method is stability-safe.\n39\t\n40\t### Stage B — stable scatter → `sorted_token_indices[N]`\n41\tGiven `expert_offsets`, place each flat index into its expert's slice in ascending-index order.\n42\t**Scheme S1 (expert-parallel sequential scan)** — decisive stability property:\n43\t- Grid = 256 programs, program `e` owns expert `e`.\n44\t- `base = expert_offsets[e]`; `running = 0`.\n45\t- Loop over `flat` in tiles of `BLOCK`, in **ascending index order**:\n46\t  - `v = load(flat + off, mask)`; `m = (v == e) & mask` (masked/tail lanes never match).\n47\t  - `excl = tl.cumsum(m.to(int32)) - m.to(int32)`  (exclusive within-tile prefix).\n48\t  - `pos = base + running + excl`; `store(out + pos, off, mask=m)`  (`off` = ascending flat index).\n49\t  - `running += tl.sum(m.to(int32))`.\n50\t- Correctness: tiles ascending × lanes-within-tile ascending ⇒ matched indices written in strictly\n51\t  ascending order ⇒ **stable by construction**. Each program writes only `[base, base+count)` —\n52\t  disjoint ranges ⇒ **no output atomics, no write races**, fully deterministic.\n53\t- Cost: O(E·N) compares (~4.7M), but `flat` is L2-resident and reused by all 256 programs ⇒\n54\t  L2-bandwidth-bound; 256 blocks over 108 SMs ≈ 2–3 blocks/SM.\n55\t\n56\tContingency **Scheme S2 (tiled counting sort, O(N) reads)** is held in reserve (see §3, c00x) only\n57\tif evidence shows Stage B dominates and the E·N reads are the bottleneck.\n58\t\n59\t**Rejected:** atomic-counter scatter (`pos = atomicAdd(write_pos[e],1)`) — non-stable ⇒ exact-match\n60\tfail. Never used."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7604146c-ed96-4f13-996e-fef77b8439f9","uuid":"1e80e31c-f9ab-4028-b99f-66d1bb2ce246","timestamp":"2026-09-16T17:48:55.014Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data1/workspace/weihongren/kda-runs/formal-kda-20260916--sol_execbench--L1-058_moe_expert_token_radix_sort_with_prefix_sum/docs/plan.md","content":"# Executable Optimization Plan — L1/058 MoE Expert Token Radix Sort + Prefix Sum\n\nTarget: NVIDIA A800 (`sm_80`, Ampere). Primary implementation = **Triton**; PyTorch only for\ntensor allocation/metadata/launch plumbing. **No** Torch/CPU/NumPy/CUDA-extension computational\nfallback. Submission entry point: `solution/solution.py::run(topk_idx) -> (sorted_token_indices,\nexpert_offsets)`.\n\nThis plan operationalizes `docs/draft.md`. It defines: the fixed pipeline architecture, a sequential\ncandidate lineage with per-candidate hypotheses and accept/reject rules, correctness checks, the\nstopping criteria, and the evidence schema. **No candidate is implemented or evaluated in this\nturn.**\n\n---\n\n## 0. Problem recap (authoritative constraints)\n\n- `run(topk_idx: int32[B, S, 8]) -> (sorted_token_indices: int32[N], expert_offsets: int32[257])`\n  with `num_experts = 256`, `num_experts_per_tok = 8`, `N = B*S*8`.\n- Semantics to reproduce **exactly** (integer, exact-match comparison; atol=1e-5/rtol=0.01 on ints):\n  1. `flat = topk_idx.reshape(-1)` (C-order), length `N`, values in `[0,255]`.\n  2. `sorted_token_indices = argsort(flat, stable=True)` — **stable** counting sort permutation:\n     within each expert bucket the original flat indices appear in **strictly ascending** order.\n  3. `expert_offsets[0] = 0`; `expert_offsets[e+1] = sum_{j<=e} count[j]` (inclusive prefix of the\n     256-bin histogram). `expert_offsets[e]:expert_offsets[e+1]` = expert `e`'s output slice.\n- Feedback workloads (5 fixed): `N ∈ {17920, 18432, 17408, 16384, 16384}` — all ~16–18k, keys\n  uniform over 256 experts (mean bucket ≈ 64–72). Flat array ≈ 64–72 KB → fully L2-resident on A800.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fixed pipeline architecture\n\nTwo logical stages with a hard cross-stage dependency (offsets must be complete before scatter can\ncompute bucket bases), so ≥2 kernel launches are required.\n\n### Stage A — histogram + prefix sum → `expert_offsets[257]`\nCompute the 256-bin histogram of `flat`, then an inclusive prefix sum, writing\n`expert_offsets[0]=0` and `expert_offsets[1:257]=inclusive_cumsum`. Purely order-independent\n(commutative counting), so any histogram method is stability-safe.\n\n### Stage B — stable scatter → `sorted_token_indices[N]`\nGiven `expert_offsets`, place each flat index into its expert's slice in ascending-index order.\n**Scheme S1 (expert-parallel sequential scan)** — decisive stability property:\n- Grid = 256 programs, program `e` owns expert `e`.\n- `base = expert_offsets[e]`; `running = 0`.\n- Loop over `flat` in tiles of `BLOCK`, in **ascending index order**:\n  - `v = load(flat + off, mask)`; `m = (v == e) & mask` (masked/tail lanes never match).\n  - `excl = tl.cumsum(m.to(int32)) - m.to(int32)`  (exclusive within-tile prefix).\n  - `pos = base + running + excl`; `store(out + pos, off, mask=m)`  (`off` = ascending flat index).\n  - `running += tl.sum(m.to(int32))`.\n- Correctness: tiles ascending × lanes-within-tile ascending ⇒ matched indices written in strictly\n  ascending order ⇒ **stable by construction**. Each program writes only `[base, base+count)` —\n  disjoint ranges ⇒ **no output atomics, no write races**, fully deterministic.\n- Cost: O(E·N) compares (~4.7M), but `flat` is L2-resident and reused by all 256 programs ⇒\n  L2-bandwidth-bound; 256 blocks over 108 SMs ≈ 2–3 blocks/SM.\n\nContingency **Scheme S2 (tiled counting sort, O(N) reads)** is held in reserve (see §3, c00x) only\nif evidence shows Stage B dominates and the E·N reads are the bottleneck.\n\n**Rejected:** atomic-counter scatter (`pos = atomicAdd(write_pos[e],1)`) — non-stable ⇒ exact-match\nfail. Never used.","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":302}}}
